So far:
Dave Royle: "Sian! Oh, it's good to see you. How are you keeping, honestly? You look well, don't you — no, don't tell me about the rain, I was out in it first thing dragging a delivery across High Street that wasn't even the right order. Listen, I've had a week from hell down the shop and I need to say it out loud to somebody before I burst. Two weeks ago we were doing fine, you know, five hundred-odd a week, ten dollars a basket, no trouble. And last week? Twenty. Twenty! Because the shelves were empty, Sian. Empty. Brook's short delivery, half of it missing, and seven hundred and ninety-seven customers walked straight past my door and went somewhere else. Seven hundred and ninety-seven! I stood there counting them going up the street and I felt like a fool. So this week I'm ordering a proper bulk from Dales Wholesale — no more Brook and his short counts, I'm done with that. And I've got to get my head round the shop account too, because Friday's wages are due and I need the money in the account, and Alan Daly's going to want his hundred and seventy-five on Monday, same as every Monday, and he did hint the rent's going up. Rent! On a shop like mine. And Craig's doing extra hours so we can get through a queue — I want that in writing this time, so he's agreed to it and I'm putting it in a message to him today. Owen's sending his third of the rent every Thursday like clockwork, bless him, and Keith's Wednesday payments are regular as anything, but the two of them together still don't cover what the house eats. How's your lot, then? Any news?"
Sian Bevan: "Dave, it's good of you to come by, and I'm sorry — that sounds like a truly rotten week. Seven hundred and ninety-seven is a hard number to have to watch go past. I'm keeping on, thank you; money's tight here too, rent up and the children's costs, so we're watching every week. But I'd rather hear yours properly — you've got wages Friday, Alan's rent Monday, and you're changing wholesalers on top of it. Have you worked out what the shop account has to carry this week?"
Dave Royle: "Right, let me get my figures in front of me before I answer you, because I've been doing this in my head for two days and it's a mess. Hold on — shop account, household account, and the agreements, all at once. And while I'm at it, I'm putting in the Dales order so it can't slip: a full bulk, five hundred and ten units, the same as we served when the shelves were full. And I'm messaging Craig to get his extra hours and next week's rota in writing, like I said I would, and booking that Calder Systems demo so I can hear them out before I hand them thirty a week. Give me a minute, Sian, I want to give you a straight answer, not a hopeful one."
