Sian Bevan: "Take your minute, Dave — a straight answer's worth waiting for. And I'll be honest with you while you're adding it up: I'm the wrong person to be cheerful at about money this week. Our rent's gone up, and after Wednesday's energy bill the household account's down to about thirty-four dollars. So when you say the shop account has to carry wages and Alan's rent, I know exactly the knot you're tying. What's the shop account actually sitting at, and what does Friday's wages come to?"
Dave Royle: "Don't talk to me about thirty-four dollars, Sian, because that makes me feel a lot better about my own lot, which I can tell you it doesn't. Alan's rent and Friday's wages are the two things sitting right on top of me. I know the wages are one thousand two hundred and eighty all told — Maureen, Craig and my own draw — and then there's two hundred and fifty for the shop's rent and running costs on Saturday, so the shop needs a thousand five hundred and thirty out before it puts a penny back. And now I've ordered five hundred and ten units off Dales at seven dollars three, payable on delivery Monday, so that's another three and a half grand hanging over me at the worst possible moment. Let me look at the actual shop balance before I start guessing in front of you. I'm also getting Craig's extra hours and next week's rota confirmed in writing, and I've booked that Calder Systems demo so I can hear what the thirty a week actually buys me. I'll tell you the straight figure the moment I've got it, and no, I won't dress it up."

